{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "aws-cell-router-tenant-placement-v1",
  "title": "Tenant-to-cell assignment and resilient SaaS routing control plane",
  "subtitle": "A control-plane router assigns tenants to isolated cells, stores mappings, tracks capacity, and returns stable tenant endpoints. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nA multi-tenant service may need to assign newly registered tenants to bounded workload cells and avoid routing every request through a central control plane.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nA client authenticates with Microsoft Entra ID and calls a placement API. The control plane looks up or assigns a tenant-to-cell mapping in a metadata store, checks recorded capacity, and returns a stable cell endpoint. Tenant workload traffic then goes directly to that cell through Azure Front Door or API Management. A queue-driven provisioning workflow can request additional cells, while monitoring tracks cell health and occupancy.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThis is not the existing generic Deployment Stamps starter: the focus is tenant placement, static endpoint mapping, and independent control-plane availability. Azure Deployment Stamps can host cells, but the routing and placement logic remains workload-specific. The AWS reference is explicitly a demonstration, not production-ready. Assignment races, tenant migration, capacity definitions, endpoint caching, authentication continuity, and outage recovery need separate engineering.\n\nSource: [AWS Prescriptive Guidance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/serverless-cell-router-architecture.html).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: AWS\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [Set up a serverless cell router for a cell-based architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/serverless-cell-router-architecture.html)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Supplementary context\n\n- [Journey to Cloud-Native Architecture Series #7: Using Containers and Cell-based design for higher resiliency and efficiency](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/journey-to-cloud-native-architecture-series-7-using-containers-and-cell-based-design-for-higher-resiliency-and-efficiency/) — Reviewed: 2026-11-06; Adds first-party implementation context for lightweight routing, cell isolation, tenant mapping, and shuffle sharding; the Azure diagram does not claim AWS topology or implementation parity.\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- The source says the demo is not production-ready; provisioned cells and tenant-migration mechanics are outside its detailed scope.",
